// @check
// @error return type mismatch: expected K, got string (the return of `addT` at the call's argument types)
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D44, WHICH HAD TWO ROOTS — and this fixture is
// what is left of it after D721 retired the other one. It was
// `tests/cases/generics/error-list-concat-no-core.vl` until then.
//
// ROOT ONE IS CLOSED, AND WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS NOW IS ONE LEVEL OUT. Two literal unions
// concatenate: `K + K` is a `string`, at the direct spelling and at the pin alike, so
// `litUnionDirect` below type-checks and the operator no longer refuses either one (D1739).
// The divergence moved to the RETURN. `addT<T>(a: T, b: T): T` declares its result to be the
// hole, and at `T = K` the body concatenates to `string` — so the instance is ill-typed, and
// the one `@error` above is the pin saying so in the same words the direct spelling uses for
// `function f(a: K, b: K): K`. The tail expression is what makes it load-bearing: an implicit
// return records the pin constraint only because it was given the `else` branch its explicit
// `return` twin already had, and without that this file is check-clean invalid wasm.
//
// ROOT TWO IS GONE, AND IT WAS NEVER A RULE. It read: there is exactly ONE concat core
// (`emitListConcatI`, hard-wired to the i32 backing), so an f64/f32/i64/u8 list, a struct
// list, a map list, a closure list and every nested list are refused at `vl check` with
// `` `+` over X has no lowering ``. That sentence concedes the program is type-valid, which
// under the standing goal is never a valid answer — and the rule's CONTENT was an accident of
// which element types one hard-wired core happened to cover, which is why it was wrong in
// both directions twice (D102: three arms too WIDE, then every rep with a wrapper too
// NARROW). A concat inspects no element; `emitListConcatG` needs a list's wrapper and backing
// heap types and nothing else, and every list rep has both. D721.
//
// WHERE ITS CELLS WENT: `tests/cases/arrays/list-concat-every-rep.vl` runs all of them and
// reads every element back by VALUE, and `literal-unions/litunion-list-concat-backing.vl`
// still pins the seven reps that ran before, unchanged.

type K = "a" | "b"

function addT<T>(a: T, b: T): T { a + b }

// The pin and the direct spelling, one line apart, on the operand shape where they disagreed.
function litUnionPinned(a: K, b: K): string { addT(a, b) }
function litUnionDirect(a: K, b: K): string { a + b }

print(litUnionPinned("a", "b"))
print(litUnionDirect("a", "b"))
